deep.reconstruction:
This page contains no mathematics. It is a printed, partly handwritten membership card ('Karte' No. 5) of the Literarisches Museum in Göttingen, a reading society or club with a reading room for journals and newspapers.

The handwritten fill-ins are:
- 'Stud. math. Riemann' (student of mathematics);
- 'Halb' in 'Halbjahre' (half-year);
- 'Ostern' (Easter) to 'Michaelis 1849' (Michaelmas, 29 September);
- the date '10ten April 1849';
- a signature, 'E. v. Leutsch', on behalf of the board (Vorstand). This is very probably the classical philologist Ernst von Leutsch, professor at Göttingen.

The card admits Riemann as an 'ausserordentliches Mitglied' (extraordinary member, the usual status for students) for the summer semester 1849. The printed note at the bottom says that on receipt of the card the society's attendant (Aufwärter) receives a fee. The amount 'Drei' (three) Thaler Prussian Courant is entered in a boxed or hatched field.

Historically, the card matches the known biography. Riemann spent 1847–49 in Berlin and returned to Göttingen at Easter 1849 to work toward his doctorate. Easter Sunday 1849 fell on 8 April, so the card was issued two days after Easter, at the start of the semester. The '40' in pencil at the upper right is the archival folio number. There is no derivation to reconstruct.
